90673828125, -0.0002925395965576172, -0.0233154296875, 0.005306243896484375, -0.01422882080078125, 0.01337432861328125, 0.0057220458984375, 0.0183258056640625, -0.0296478271484375, 0.022552490234375, 0.01236724853515625, 0.0157012939453125, -0.0050811767578125, -0.0206146240234375, 0.004482269287109375, -0.006473541259765625, 0.0254058837890625, -0.0250701904296875, -0.003932952880859375, -0.007671356201171875, -0.0303192138671875, 0.0032711029052734375, -0.007175445556640625, -0.0017747879028320312, -0.01080322265625, 0.040802001953125, 0.00460052490234375, 0.00907135009765625, -0.01560211181640625, -0.002300262451171875, 0.019805908203125, 0.005870819091796875, 0.00274658203125, -0.00786590576171875, -0.0255889892578125, 0.006954193115234375, -0.0280303955078125, -0.0009684562683105469, -0.0085601806640625, -0.0233612060546875, 0.0328369140625, -0.0374755859375, 0.004001617431640625, -0.032379150390625, -0.0200653076171875, 0.01444244384765625, -0.022064208984375, -0.0138397216796875, -0.0107269287109375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. However, the reliability aspects of these methods, such as their consistency, robustness to changes in task order, and reproducibility of improvements, have been critically overlooked. Reliability is crucial for real-world deployment, where agents must perform consistently across varying conditions and task sequences.\n\nIn this work, we conduct a comprehensive re-evaluation of two memory-based methods, broadening the scope of evaluation along two axes: (1) including multiple runs to quantify variance, and (2) randomly shuffling the tasks to investigate the effect of task order. These axes were chosen because, in practical deployments, agents may encounter tasks in unpredictable orders and must deliver stable performance across repeated uses. Our experiments are conducted in environments with complex dynamics and multi-step tasks, such as simulated household chores requiring sequential actions and decision-making under uncertainty.\n\nThrough these experiments, we make two observations that expose the fragility of current methods: First, agent evaluation is inherently noisy in complex environments and on multi-step tasks\u2014where complexity refers to environments with stochastic elements, long-term dependencies, or ambiguous feedback\u2014and stacking a self-improving loop on top can further amplify this noise. Second, the agent's improvement is highly dependent on task order. Prior works often adopt default orderings that impose an implicit curriculum, acting as a hidden prerequisite for success.\n\nTo better understand this fragility, we manually examine the agents' memory by reviewing the stored textual entries after each task, identifying patterns in what information is retained or omitted, and noting inconsistencies or gaps in the memory content. We hypothesize that task and environment underspecification\u2014where the task instructions or environment feedback lack sufficient detail to fully guide agent behavior\u2014contribute to this fragility. For example, a task may not specify all necessary steps, or the environment may provide ambiguous signals about success or failure.\n\nWe validate this hypothesis by incorporating information that enables better specification, such as detailed rubrics outlining success criteria and explicit environment feedback, into the memory construction process. This intervention led to a measurable reduction in performance variance and improved average success rates by 10\u201315% in our experiments, though significant gaps still remain, suggesting that other uncharacterized factors contribute to this fragility.\n\nLooking ahead, our work advocates for more rigorous evaluation protocols for self-improving agents, such as reporting results across multiple runs, testing under randomized task orders, and including stress tests in challenging environments. Moreover, our findings on underspecification call for systems and interfaces that enable effective human oversight, such as tools for reviewing and editing agent memory or mechanisms for injecting clarifying information, to prevent agents from failing in unforeseeable ways."}}